.Dd September 30, 2026
.Dt KLDLOAD-ROLLBACK 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kldload-rollback ,
.Nm rollback
.Nd boot the system as it was before a package transaction, from a snapshot of its boot environment
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm rollback
.Op Fl -no-reboot
.Nm rollback
.Cm last | to Ar snapshot
.Op Fl -no-reboot
.Nm rollback
.Cm list | status | cancel
.Nm rollback
.Cm activate Ar environment
.Nm rollback
.Cm migrate-pkgdb
.Nm rollback
.Cm help | Fl h | Fl -help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The apt and dnf hooks snapshot the boot environment \(em the root
dataset under
.Ar pool Ns Pa /ROOT
\(em before every transaction.
.Nm rollback
clones one of those snapshots into a new boot environment, points the
pool's
.Ql bootfs
at it and restarts the machine into it.
.Pp
It clones rather than running
.Xr zfs-rollback 8 ,
which cannot act on a mounted root and destroys every snapshot newer than
its target.
The environment rolled away from is left as it was, and until the restart
.Cm cancel
undoes everything.
Nothing is ever destroyed.
.Pp
Only the root moves: home directories, other datasets and volumes are
left alone.
The clone gets the boot environment's properties, among them the
ZFSBootMenu kernel command line, which a clone does not otherwise
inherit.
On a Secure Boot install, where GRUB boots a signed kernel from the EFI
system partition rather than through ZFSBootMenu, it also copies the
clone's kernel and initramfs there and rewrites
.Pa grub.cfg ,
keeping the old ones to put back on
.Cm cancel .
It refuses before changing anything when the snapshot does not exist,
when the partition has no room, or when the package database lives
outside the boot environment, since the files would then go back and
the database would not.
.Pp
It also answers as
.Ql apt rollback
and
.Ql dnf rollback ,
through
.Xr kldload-pkg-wrapper 8 .
Everything but
.Cm help ,
.Cm list
and
.Cm status
needs root.
.Sh COMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Nm rollback
Show the snapshots, numbered, and ask which one; the default is the
newest taken before a transaction.
Then as
.Cm to .
With no terminal it changes nothing and exits 2: it will not restart a
machine for someone who was shown no question.
.It Cm last
The newest snapshot taken before a transaction
.Pq Ql apt-pre- , Ql dnf-pre- , Ql kpkg- ;
never one taken after, which holds the state being left.
.It Cm to Ar snapshot
That snapshot, by the name
.Cm list
shows or in full.
.It Cm list
The snapshots of the running environment, oldest first, with who took
each.
.It Cm status
Which environment is running, which boots next, how it boots, whether a
rollback is staged, how many snapshots there are, and how many platform
packages are pinned.
.It Cm cancel
Point
.Ql bootfs ,
and on the Secure Boot path the boot files, back at the running
environment.
.It Cm activate Ar environment
Boot an existing environment next, the same way; never restarts.
.Xr kbe 8
uses this.
.It Cm migrate-pkgdb
For pools installed before the package database was part of the boot
environment: copy it in, and stop the separate dataset from mounting.
Takes one restart.
.El
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl n , Fl -no-reboot
Stage the rollback and stop; it takes effect whenever the machine next
starts.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev KLDLOAD_ROLLBACK_REBOOT
0 is
.Fl -no-reboot .
.It Ev KLDLOAD_ROLLBACK_DELAY
Seconds counted down before the restart; default 2.
Interrupting the countdown leaves the rollback staged.
.It Ev KLDLOAD_ROLLBACK_ALLOW_SPLIT_PKGDB
1 goes ahead although the package database is outside the boot
environment.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /var/log/kldload/rollback.log
Every action.
.It Pa /var/lib/kldload/esp-backup/
The Secure Boot path's kernel, initramfs and
.Pa grub.cfg
as they were before a rollback was staged, with the environment they
came from;
.Cm cancel
restores them only into that environment, then removes them.
.It Pa /var/lib/kldload/platform-holds.list
Read by
.Cm status .
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width 1n -compact
.It 0
Done, or nothing chosen.
.It 1
Refused or failed, named; a failure after
.Ql bootfs
moved says so and names
.Cm cancel .
.It 2
An unknown command, or a bare
.Nm rollback
with no terminal; nothing changed.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
